  "subject": "Basic Electrical Engineering",
  "topic": "Difference between Resistance and Impedance in DC and AC Circuits",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Why does resistance become impedance the moment electricity alternates?",
  "question": "In a steady-state direct current circuit, how do pure inductors and capacitors behave compared to alternating current circuits?",
  "options": [
   "Inductor acts as an open circuit and capacitor acts as a short circuit",
   "Inductor acts as a short circuit and capacitor acts as an open circuit",
   "Both behave as pure resistances equal to their respective reactances",
   "Both offer infinite impedance and completely block the steady direct current"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 1,
  "answerLine": "The inductor acts as a short circuit while the capacitor acts as an open circuit.",
  "explanation": [
   "Resistance is opposition to current flow caused purely by collisions of electrons in a material.",
   "In direct current circuits, the supply frequency is zero, so opposition depends strictly on ohmic resistance.",
   "Inductive reactance equals two pi times frequency times inductance, so it drops to zero at zero hertz.",
   "Therefore, a pure inductor offers zero opposition and behaves simply as a short circuit in direct current."
  ],
  "funFact": "Oliver Heaviside coined the term impedance in eighteen eighty-six to describe total opposition to alternating currents.",
