  "fingerprint": "In a steady-state direct current circuit, how do pure inductors and capacitors behave compared to alternating current circuits?~~Inductor acts as an open circuit and capacitor acts as a short circuit|Inductor acts as a short circuit and capacitor acts as an open circuit|Both behave as pure resistances equal to their respective reactances|Both offer infinite impedance and completely block the steady direct current~~1~~Resistance is opposition to current flow caused purely by collisions of electrons in a material.|In direct current circuits, the supply frequency is zero, so opposition depends strictly on ohmic resistance.|Inductive reactance equals two pi times frequency times inductance, so it drops to zero at zero hertz.|Therefore, a pure inductor offers zero opposition and behaves simply as a short circuit in direct current.~~Resistance is opposition to current flow caused purely by collisions of electrons in a material.|In direct current circuits, the supply frequency is zero, so opposition depends strictly on ohmic resistance.|Inductive reactance equals two pi times frequency times inductance, so it drops to zero at zero hertz.|Therefore, a pure inductor offers zero opposition and behaves simply as a short circuit in direct current.|Capacitive reactance equals one divided by two pi times frequency times capacitance, approaching infinity at direct current.|Consequently, a capacitor completely blocks steady direct current, functioning as an open circuit after charging.|In alternating current circuits, alternating magnetic and electric fields create inductive and capacitive reactances that resist current.|Impedance is the total phasor combination of real resistance and net reactive opposition in alternating current circuits.|Because alternating current faces both energy loss and field storage, total opposition is named impedance, symbol Z."
